B.W6=new A.c9("Landau-Kleffner syndrome","Rare / genetic syndrome","Acquired epileptic aphasia \u2014 a rare childhood disorder with progressive loss of language comprehension/expression associated with an epileptiform EEG, with or without overt seizures.",B.Nv,"Near-continuous spike-wave activity during slow-wave sleep, often bitemporal.","Unexplained language regression in a young child warrants a sleep EEG to look for Landau-Kleffner syndrome, even without witnessed seizures.",!0)
B.MZ=s(["Onset within the first 3 months, often the first days of life","Frequent tonic spasms, sometimes clustering","Severe developmental impairment","May evolve into West syndrome and later Lennox-Gastaut syndrome"],t.s)
B.VZ=new A.c9("Ohtahara syndrome","Rare / genetic syndrome","Early infantile epileptic encephalopathy presenting in the first weeks of life with tonic spasms and a burst-suppression EEG pattern; among the earliest-onset and most severe epilepsy syndromes.",B.MZ,"Burst-suppression pattern (bursts of high-voltage spikes alternating with near-flat suppression), present in both wakefulness and sleep.","A burst-suppression EEG in a neonate with tonic spasms should prompt urgent evaluation for a structural or metabolic cause of Ohtahara syndrome.",!0)
B.Mx=s(["Usual onset in childhood","Frequent, drug-resistant focal motor seizures","Progressive unilateral weakness and cognitive decline over months to years","Hemispherotomy/hemispherectomy is the most effective treatment in confirmed cases"],t.s)
B.W8=new A.c9("Rasmussen encephalitis","Rare / genetic syndrome","A rare, progressive inflammatory disease of one cerebral hemisphere causing drug-resistant focal seizures, often epilepsia partialis continua, with progressive hemiparesis and cognitive decline.",B.Mx,"Progressive unihemispheric slowing with multifocal epileptiform discharges over the affected hemisphere.","Progressive unilateral weakness accompanying drug-resistant focal seizures in a child should raise suspicion for Rasmussen encephalitis and prompt MRI for unihemispheric atrophy.",!0)
B.NL=s(["Continuous or near-continuous focal clonic jerking, classically of a hand or face","Awareness usually preserved","Can persist for hours to years despite treatment","Causes include Rasmussen encephalitis, stroke, tumor, and metabolic derangement (e.g. non-ketotic hyperglycemia)"],t.s)
B.Wh=new A.c9("Epilepsia partialis continua","Rare / genetic syndrome","A rare form of focal motor status epilepticus with continuous, rhythmic clonic jerking of a body part, sometimes lasting days to years, usually with preserved awareness.",B.NL,"May show focal epileptiform discharges time-locked to the jerking, but scalp EEG is often unremarkable given the small cortical generator.","A normal scalp EEG does not exclude epilepsia partialis continua \u2014 the diagnosis is primarily clinical.",!0)
B.NT=s(["Clusters of brief (seconds to a minute), stereotyped hypermotor/hyperkinetic seizures during sleep","Onset usually in childhood, often with a family history","Frequently confused with NREM parasomnias (sleepwalking, night terrors)","Preserved awareness is common, distinguishing it from typical parasomnias"],t.s)
B.W2=new A.c9("Autosomal dominant nocturnal frontal lobe epilepsy (ADNFLE)","Rare / genetic syndrome","A genetic (often CHRNA4/CHRNB2 nicotinic receptor gene) focal epilepsy causing brief, clustered hypermotor seizures during sleep, frequently misdiagnosed as a parasomnia.",B.NT,"Scalp EEG is frequently normal or non-localizing given the frontal, often mesial, seizure onset.","A strong family history of stereotyped nocturnal events favors ADNFLE over an isolated parasomnia.",!0)
B.Ny=s(["Peak onset age 3-6 years","Prominent ictal autonomic symptoms: vomiting, pallor, eye deviation","Seizures can be prolonged (autonomic status epilepticus), even though isolated events are otherwise benign","Usually resolves spontaneously within a few years"],t.s)
B.Wb=new A.c9("Panayiotopoulos syndrome","Rare / genetic syndrome","A self-limited childhood focal epilepsy dominated by autonomic ictal features \u2014 most strikingly prominent vomiting \u2014 usually during sleep, with an excellent long-term prognosis.",B.Ny,"Multifocal, often occipital, spikes; interictal EEG can also be normal.","Recurrent nocturnal vomiting with impaired awareness in a young child is a classic, under-recognized presentation of Panayiotopoulos syndrome, not gastroenteritis.",!0)
